          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Jack:</strong> How is data stored?</p>
<p>Data storage allows data to be stored on a computer when it powers down. All data in a computer is stored as a number, but not a conventional number. Conventional numbers use ten different digits but binary numbers use two digits only.</p>
<p>Data is converted into binary digital form using two digits 1 and 0 to be stored.</p>
<p>A bit is the smallest unit of data representing a single value.</p>
<p>A byte is eight binary digits long.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> The conventional numbers 0-8 translate into 0, 1, 10, 11, 100, 101, 110, 111 and 1000.</p>
<p>So when data is stored even letters become numbers, for example the words in a book would be stored one byte per letter. Photographs are also converted to numbers that indicate the location, colour and brightness of each pixel.</p>
<p><strong>Jack:</strong> Storage and memory is often measured in megabytes and gigabytes and even terabytes, however, as units of data measurement begin to grow so does the amount of data collected and stored.</p>
<p>Binary data is primarily stored on the hard disk drive [HDD]. In addition to this storage can be on SSD flash drives and memory cards. With this type of storage, the binary numbers are stored as a series of electrical charges within NAND flash chips.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> To store data regardless of its form, users, need to use storage devices. These will be either 'direct area' storage or 'network based' storage.</p>
<p>Direct area storage is directly attached to a computer. It can offer back up services or can be used as storage in conjunction with a program that may be memory intensive.</p>
<p>Network based storage allows more than one computer to access it through the network making it better for sharing and collaborating. It may be off site or even cloud based.</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
